The Rust translation of the [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m function itself was correct — the blake256 implementation, the buffer construction, and the internal logic all matched the C original. The problem was a [1mmissing C ABI symbol export[22m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
In the C code, the header [38;5;10mhash.h[0m uses a namespace macro:[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m#define prf_addr SPX_NAMESPACE(prf_addr)  // expands to SPX_prf_addr
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
So the compiled C library exports the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m. The test runner ([38;5;10mcando2[0m harness) dynamically loads the [38;5;10m.so[0m and looks up this exact symbol name. The Rust library had the internal [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m function in [38;5;10mhash_blake.rs[0m but never exported it as [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m with C linkage.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fix was adding this wrapper to [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_prf_addr(
    out: *mut u8,
    ctx: *const context::SpxCtx,
    addr: *const [u32; 8],
) {
    let ctx = unsafe { &*ctx };
    let addr = unsafe { &*addr };
    let out = unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(out, params::SPX_N) };
    hash_blake::prf_addr(out, ctx, addr);
